WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
Stemming.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.170
A search for failures should also find failed and failing.

3
00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.133
Stemming is the cheap trick that makes that happen.

4
00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:12.867
Stemming chops suffixes off words using hand-written rules.

5
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:17.981
The classic Porter stemmer, from nineteen eighty, has no dictionary and no grammar.

6
00:00:17.981 --> 00:00:18.967
Just a rule list.

7
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:21.810
Its output doesn't have to be a real word.

8
00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.567
It only has to be consistent.

9
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.900
Here's what the rules do to real words.

10
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.662
Running becomes run.

11
00:00:28.662 --> 00:00:29.100
Clean.

12
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.573
Engines becomes engin, and studies becomes studi.

13
00:00:32.573 --> 00:00:35.033
Not words, but always the same output.

14
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.220
And sometimes it's plain wrong.

15
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:38.133
Was becomes wa.

16
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:41.149
So why accept ugly output?

17
00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:42.267
Follow a search.

18
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:44.100
The query says telemetry.

19
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.833
The stemmer chops it down.

20
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:49.633
The document says telemetric, and it gets stemmed the same way.

21
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:53.741
Both land on the same string, telemetri, and now they match.

22
00:00:53.741 --> 00:00:55.200
That's recall going up.

23
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.291
The danger is over-stemming.

24
00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:03.423
Universal and university both become univers, so unrelated words get merged.

25
00:01:03.423 --> 00:01:06.867
Under-stemming misses forms in the other direction.

26
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:12.033
The fix is to keep stems hidden inside the index, and never show them to users.

27
00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:14.800
Here it is with NLTK.

28
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.238
We create a Porter stemmer and grab its stem function.

29
00:01:18.238 --> 00:01:19.400
Nothing to download.

30
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.067
Then we loop over the words and print each one beside its stem.

31
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:25.433
Where does this show up?

32
00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:29.967
Your Mission Control RAG has a keyword search leg next to the vector leg.

33
00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:36.567
Stem its index, and a query for pressurization failures matches pressurized, pressurizing, and failure.

34
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:41.200
Recall jumps with zero model cost, and the stems never reach the user.

35
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:43.185
Let's recap.

36
00:01:43.185 --> 00:01:46.000
Stemming chops suffixes using simple rules.

37
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.900
Its output only needs to be consistent, not a real word.

38
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.200
It's great inside search indexes and bad anywhere a user reads it.

39
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.495
And watch for over-stemming.

40
00:01:56.495 --> 00:01:58.133
Next up: lemmatization.
